## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about I/O efficiency, batching, and dynamic graph algorithms under memory constraints; blends theory with practical concerns like latency and cache locality.

## Key Concepts

- External memory I/O model (M, B) and batch updates
- Dynamic connectivity with batched insertions
- Amortized analysis and rebuild strategies
- Spanning forests and residual edges
- Locality-aware data partitioning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: batch I/O efficient dynamic connectivity
class DynConn {
  constructor(T){ this.F = new Forest(); this.buffer = [];
  addBatch(edges){
    this.buffer.push(edges);
    if(this.buffer.length * b >= T) this.rebuild();
  }
  connected(u,v){
    return this.F.find(u) === this.F.find(v) || 
           this.searchResidual(u,v);
  }
  rebuild(){
    // merge buffered edges into compact structure
    // amortized O((n+mb)/B) I/Os
  }
}
```

## Performance Analysis

**(i) Data Structure:** Maintain spanning forest in RAM, buffered edges on disk with locality-aware partitioning, and periodic rebuild of compact connectivity structure.

**(ii) Amortized I/O:** O((n + mb)/B) per batch, where rebuild occurs every T/b batches.

**(iii) Query Time:** O(log_B n) average case, O(m/B) worst case when searching residual edges.

**(iv) Space:** O(n + m + T) where T is buffer threshold, typically Θ(n + m).

**(v) Optimizations:** 1) Adaptive rebuild threshold based on update/query ratio; 2) Multi-level buffering with hot/cold edge classification.

**(vi) Comparison:** Recomputing from scratch costs O((n+m)α(n,m)/B) per batch vs. O((n+mb)/B) amortized with batching—significant improvement when b << m.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental triangle counting under streaming edge additions; evaluates ability to design batched analysis and compare to full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental graph algorithms
- Degree-based edge orientation
- Wedge counting and triangle updates
- Amortized analysis
- Space-time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function addEdge(u,v,adj,deg,tri){
  if (deg[u] > deg[v]) [u,v] = [v,u];
  let cnt = 0;
  for (const w of adj[u]){
    if (adj[v].has(w)) cnt++;
  }
  tri.total += cnt;
  adj[u].add(v); adj[v].add(u);
  deg[u]++; deg[v]++;
}
```

## Further Discussion
This batched approach significantly outperforms rebuilding from scratch, which would require O(n³) time in the worst case for triangle enumeration. The incremental method achieves near-linear preprocessing and maintains efficient updates, making it practical for large-scale dynamic graphs where edges arrive continuously.

## Why This Is Asked
Explores practical dynamic approximate all-pairs shortest paths (APSP) with shared structure; tests understanding of trade-offs between update costs and query time in batch-dynamic settings.

## Key Concepts
- Batch-dynamic graphs with nonnegative edge weights
- Hub-based distance sketches for approximate APSP
- Amortized analysis across multiple query pairs
- Trade-offs between approximation accuracy ε and computational efficiency
- Incremental shortest path algorithms

## Code Example
```javascript
// Hub-based distance approximation: dist(u,v) ≈ min_h∈H [dist(u,h) + dist(h,v)]

class HubBasedAPSP {
  constructor(graph, epsilon) {
    this.k = Math.ceil((1/epsilon) * Math.log(graph.n));
    this.hubs = this.selectHubs(graph);
    this.distances = new Map(); // hub -> vertex distances
  }

  updateBatch(edges) {
    for (const hub of this.hubs) {
      this.distances.set(hub, this.incrementalDijkstra(hub, edges));
    }
  }

  query(u, v) {
    let minDist = Infinity;
    for (const hub of this.hubs) {
      const dist = this.distances.get(hub).get(u) + 
                   this.distances.get(hub).get(v);
      minDist = Math.min(minDist, dist);
    }
    return minDist;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design streaming top-k algorithms within a sliding window using compact probabilistic data structures and articulate clear trade-offs between accuracy, space, and time.

## Key Concepts

- **Sliding window processing**: Maintaining state only over the most recent W elements
- **Space-Saving sketch**: A fixed-memory algorithm that provides approximate frequency counts
- **Top-k aggregation**: Identifying the k most frequent items in a stream
- **Approximation guarantees**: Bounding error in frequency estimates
- **Time/space complexity**: Analyzing per-operation and batch performance

## Code Example

```javascript
class SlidingTopK {
  constructor(k, W) {
    this.k = k;
    this.W = W;
    this.counters = new Map(); // item -> count
    this.window = []; // queue of last W items
  }

  add(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
    } else if (this.counters.size < this.k) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
    } else {
      const minEntry = this.findMinCounter();
      this.counters.delete(minEntry.key);
      this.counters.set(x, minEntry.value + 1);
    }
    this.window.push(x);
    if (this.window.length > this.W) {
      this.expire(this.window.shift());
    }
  }

  expire(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      const newCount = this.counters.get(x) - 1;
      if (newCount > 0) {
        this.counters.set(x, newCount);
      } else {
        this.counters.delete(x);
      }
    }
  }

  findMinCounter() {
    let minEntry = { key: null, value: Infinity };
    for (const [key, value] of this.counters) {
      if (value < minEntry.value) {
        minEntry = { key, value };
      }
    }
    return minEntry;
  }

  topK() {
    return [...this.counters.entries()]
      .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])
      .slice(0, this.k);
  }
}
```

