## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of amortized analysis with a practical growth strategy, highlighting how linear growth increments behave differently from exponential doubling and how memory copies interact with cache locality.

## Key Concepts
- Amortized cost under linear growth
- Block-based capacity increments (capacity increases by B, not a multiplicative factor)
- Cache locality during bulk copies
- Trade-offs between growth frequency and per-copy work

## Code Example
```javascript
class Queue {
  constructor(capacity = B) {
    this.buffer = new Array(capacity);
    this.head = 0;
    this.tail = 0;
    this.size = 0;
  }

  enqueue(item) {
    if (this.size === this.buffer.length) {
      const newBuffer = new Array(this.buffer.length + B);
      for (let i = 0; i < this.size; i++) {
        newBuffer[i] = this.buffer[(this.head + i) % this.buffer.length];
      }
      this.buffer = newBuffer;
      this.head = 0;
      this.tail = this.size;
    }
    this.buffer[this.tail] = item;
    this.tail = (this.tail + 1) % this.buffer.length;
    this.size++;
  }

  dequeue() {
    if (this.size === 0) return undefined;
    const item = this.buffer[this.head];
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.buffer.length;
    this.size--;
    return item;
  }
}
```

## B's Influence
- **Total copies**: Larger B reduces growth frequency, cutting total copy work from O(N²) toward O(N) (since copies ≈ N²/(2B))
- **Cache behavior**: Smaller B keeps working set in cache but triggers more frequent expensive copies; larger B improves sequential access patterns during copies but may evict useful cache contents when reallocating

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design streaming data structures with sliding windows, handling batched updates, and rigorous amortized analysis. Also probes correctness under expirations and the practical choice between exact maintenance vs rebuilds.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window frequency tables, heap with lazy deletion, batch amortized analysis, memory locality, worst-case scenarios.

## Code Example
```javascript
class TopFreqWindow {
  constructor(W) { this.W = W; this.cnt = new Map(); this.queue = []; this.heap = new MaxHeap(); }
  batchInsert(vals) {
    for (const v of vals) {
      this.queue.push(v);
      this.cnt.set(v, (this.cnt.get(v) || 0) + 1);
      this.heap.push({ value: v, count: this.cnt.get(v) });
    }
    this.expireIfNeeded();
  }
  expireIfNeeded() {
    while (this.queue.length > this.W) {
      const old = this.queue.shift();
      const c = (this.cnt.get(old) || 0) - 1;
      if (c <= 0) this.cnt.delete(old); else this.cnt.set(old, c);
      this.heap.push({ value: old, count: c });
    }
  }
  top1() {
    while (true) {
      const t = this.heap.peek(); if (!t) return null;
      if ((this.cnt.get(t.value) || 0) === t.count) return t.value;
      this.heap.pop();
    }
  }
}
```

