## Why This Is Asked
Tests sliding-window frequency tracking with fixed memory and per-element updates, a common real-time pattern in fraud/rate-limiting use cases. It also shows practical data-structure trade-offs for beginners.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window with fixed memory
- Circular buffer + hashmap for frequencies
- Per-element O(1) updates; batch O(B)
- Threshold tracking to avoid full scans
- Choosing W, r, B depends on latency and memory constraints

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowCounter {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.buf = new Array(W);
    this.head = 0;
    this.size = 0;
    this.freq = new Map();
    this.crossed = 0; // number of IDs currently crossing the threshold
  }
  processBatch(batch, r) {
    for (const id of batch) {
      // increment
      const c = this.freq.get(id) || 0;
      const nc = c + 1;
      this.freq.set(id, nc);
      if (nc === r) this.crossed++;
      // push to circular buffer
      const idx = (this.head + this.size) % this.W;
      this.buf[idx] = id;
      if (this.size < this.W) {
        this.size++;
      } else {
        // evict oldest
        const old = this.buf[this.head];
        this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
        const co = this.freq.get(old) || 0;
        const nco = co - 1;
        if (nco <= 0) this.freq.delete(old);
        else this.freq.set(old, nco);
        if (co >= r && nco < r) this.crossed--;
      }
    }
    return this.crossed > 0;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for multiple thresholds or weighted events? 
- What are trade-offs vs Misra-Gries / Space-Saving for skewed data? 
- How would you parallelize across shards or handle bursts with variable B?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of amortized batching, range updates, and exact coverage checks on a line, common in streaming analytics and scheduling systems.

## Key Concepts
- Coordinate compression, segment tree with range updates, global min check, amortized batching, space efficiency.

## Code Example
```javascript
class SegTree {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.min=Array(4*n).fill(0); this.cover=Array(4*n).fill(0); this.lazy=Array(4*n).fill(0); }
  _apply(i,delta){ this.cover[i]+=delta; this.min[i]+=delta; this.lazy[i]+=delta; }
  _push(i){ if(this.lazy[i]!==0){ this._apply(i*2,this.lazy[i]); this._apply(i*2+1,this.lazy[i]); this.lazy[i]=0; } }
  update(qL,qR,delta,i=1,l=0,r=this.n-1){ if(qR<l||r<qL) return; if(qL<=l&&r<=qR){ this._apply(i,delta); return; } this._push(i); const m=(l+r)>>1; this.update(qL,qR,delta,i*2,l,m); this.update(qL,qR,delta,i*2+1,m+1,r); this.min[i]=Math.min(this.min[i*2],this.min[i*2+1]); }
  fullCovered(){ return this.min[1]>0; }
}
``` 

